# PHP / Laravel coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern PHP
(8.2+) and Laravel (11/12/13) idiom. Applies only to `*.php` files —
everything not PHP/Laravel-specific still comes from the common rules
this file `extends`.

## Naming and structure

- Classes, interfaces, enums, and traits are `StudlyCase`; methods and
  properties are `camelCase`; constants and enum cases are
  `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` unless the enum case is meant to read as a label.
- Follow Laravel's default directory conventions
  (`app/Models`, `app/Http/Controllers`, `app/Http/Requests`,
  `app/Providers`, `app/Jobs`, `database/migrations`) rather than
  inventing a parallel structure for the same kind of class.
- A controller method stays thin: validate (via a Form Request), delegate
  to a model/action/service, return a response — push business rules into
  a model method, an action class, or a service, not the controller.
- Type-hint every parameter, property, and return value that can be
  typed; PHP 8.2+ intersection types and `never`/`static` return types are
  fair game where they genuinely describe the contract.

## PHP 8.2+ language features

- `readonly` properties for value objects and anything constructed once
  and never mutated (a DTO, an immutable config value) instead of a
  mutable property guarded only by convention.
- Native `enum` (backed by `string`/`int` when the value needs to persist
  to a column or API payload) instead of class constants for a fixed set
  of related values.
- First-class callable syntax (`$this->method(...)`, `Str::slug(...)`)
  instead of a string/array callable (`[$this, 'method']`) or an
  unnecessary closure wrapper when passing a method as a callback.
- Constructor property promotion (`public function __construct(private
  readonly OrderRepository $orders) {}`) for simple DTOs and services
  instead of repeating each property as a separate declaration plus
  assignment.

## Eloquent models

- Declare `protected $fillable` (an explicit allowlist) or `protected
  $guarded` on every model that is ever mass-assigned; do not leave a
  model without either and rely on `Model::unguard()` outside of a
  seeder/test context.
- Cast attributes with the model's `casts()` method (or the `$casts`
  property on codebases not yet migrated to it) rather than hand-parsing
  dates/JSON/enums in accessors.
- Put a relationship's return type on the method
  (`: HasMany`, `: BelongsTo`, ...) so IDEs and static analysis can follow
  it.

## Controllers, routes, and requests

- Route parameters use route-model binding
  (`Route::get('/users/{user}', ...)` resolving to a typed `User $user`
  parameter) instead of manually calling `User::findOrFail($id)` in every
  controller method that already receives the bound model.
- Validate every request that accepts user input through a Form Request
  class (`php artisan make:request`) or `$request->validate([...])`, not
  by reading raw `$request->all()`/`$request->input()` values straight
  into model or database calls.
- Register route-specific middleware with `->middleware(...)` on the
  route/group; keep global middleware in `bootstrap/app.php`
  (or `app/Http/Kernel.php` on codebases still on that structure) rather
  than duplicating a global concern per-route.

## Formatting

- Follow PSR-12 spacing/brace conventions; run the project's configured
  formatter (`./vendor/bin/pint` is Laravel's default) instead of
  hand-formatting around one that is already configured.
- One class per file, filename matching the class name, matching the
  namespace to the directory per PSR-4 — do not add a second top-level
  class/interface/enum to an existing file.
